    "summary": "The client adopts the SCID of the first Initial it takes and discards later long-header packets with another SCID, except that an ACK-only Initial from the client's own DCID (msquic) is processed without fixing the SCID, against two MUSTs of §7.2.",
    "topic": "§7.2 — the server's Source Connection ID is fixed by the first Initial"
   },
   {
    "details": "rfc/8899.md#s3-probing-black-holes-ptb-timers",
    "rfc": "RFC 8899",
    "section": "QUIC",
    "since": "0.3.0",
    "status": "🔶",
    "summary": "Probe loss is no congestion signal, a probe fails on loss detection but not within 1 s of its sending, black holes fall back to 1200 bytes, Packet Too Big messages are validated hints, and the search repeats every 600 s.",
    "topic": "§3, §4.3, §4.6, §5.1.1 — probing, black holes, Packet Too Big, timers"
   },
   {
    "details": "rfc/6961.md#status-request-v2",
    "rfc": "RFC 6961",
    "section": "TLS",
    "since": "—",
    "status": "❌",
    "summary": "The multiple certificate status extension is deprecated by RFC 9846 and no longer recommended by RFC 9325, so it is never sent.",
    "topic": "`status_request_v2` (obsoleted by RFC 9846)"
   },
   {
    "details": "rfc/9325.md#s4-2-deterministic-ecdsa",
    "rfc": "RFC 9325",
    "section": "TLS",
    "since": "0.3.0",
    "status": "🔶",
    "summary": "Handshake ECDSA signatures use OpenSSL's nonce from the key, the digest and fresh randomness, not the RECOMMENDED RFC 6979 deterministic ECDSA, an accepted deviation.",
    "topic": "§4.2 — deterministic ECDSA"
   },
   {
    "details": "rfc/9114.md#s4-6-server-push",
    "rfc": "RFC 9114",
    "section": "HTTP",
    "since": "—",
    "status": "❌",
    "summary": "Server push is not planned; push frames are refused with the error codes of each role.",
    "topic": "§4.6, §7.2.3, §7.2.5, §7.2.7 — server push"
   },
   {
    "details": "rfc/9298.md#s3-2-http1-and-http2",
    "rfc": "RFC 9298",
    "section": "HTTP",
    "since": "—",
    "status": "❌",
    "summary": "CONNECT-UDP is offered over HTTP/3 only, where §6 says UDP proxying should run.",
    "topic": "§3.2, §3.3; §3.4, §3.5 over HTTP/2 — HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2"
   },
   {
    "details": "rfc/9113.md#s8-4-server-push",
    "rfc": "RFC 9113",
    "section": "HTTP",
    "since": "—",
    "status": "❌",
    "summary": "Server push is not planned: the client disables it and refuses PUSH_PROMISE, and the server never pushes.",
    "topic": "§6.6, §8.4 — server push"
   },
   {
    "details": "rfc/1928-1929.md#s3-gssapi",
    "rfc": "RFC 1928",
    "section": "HTTP",
    "since": "—",
    "status": "❌",
    "summary": "The GSSAPI method is not offered, so a proxy that requires it is refused.",
    "topic": "§3 — GSSAPI method"
   },
   {
    "details": "rfc/6455.md#s4-1-connecting-through-a-proxy",
    "rfc": "RFC 6455",
    "section": "HTTP",
    "since": "0.3.0",
    "status": "🔶",
    "summary": "At most one WebSocket per remote host is in the CONNECTING state and a configured proxy is used, with PAC and WPAD run by the engine itself on Apple, by WinHTTP on Windows (not verified at runtime) and by the platform selector on the JVM and Android, and a PAC script gets a `wss` URI as its origin only unless the application opts in for the script the settings name (an owner-approved security deviation from the PAC URI rule, as browsers and Android do).",
    "topic": "§4.1 (client: connecting, through a proxy) — connecting, through a proxy"
   }
